==================================================
Lab 3: Blind OS Command Injection with Output Redirection
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A website has a feedback form. When you submit it, the server runs a shell command using the details you entered.
The command's output is not shown in the response, so we cannot read it directly.
Instead, we redirect the output into a file inside a folder that the website serves publicly, then load that file through the browser.

Goal: run the whoami command and retrieve its output.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When you submit feedback, the browser sends the name, email, subject, and message to the server.
The server uses these values in a shell command, but the response page does not show the command's output.
The website also serves product images from /var/www/images/ through the /image?filename= endpoint.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The email field is placed directly into a shell command without any filtering.
That lets us add our own commands, and we can control where their output goes.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Double pipe (||): The second command runs only if the first one fails. The first part, mypunch@123, is not a valid command, so whoami runs.
Output redirection (>): The > symbol sends a command's output into a file instead of the screen.
Serving folder: /var/www/images/ is writable and publicly readable through /image, so a file we write there can be downloaded by the script.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

We put this value into the email field:

mypunch@123 ||whoami>/var/www/images/output.txt||

The server builds a command that roughly looks like:

mail mypunch@123 ||whoami>/var/www/images/output.txt||

The first part fails, so the shell runs whoami. The > sends its output to output.txt inside the images folder.
Nothing appears in the response, but the file now exists on the server.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and go to the feedback page.
Fill in the fields and enter the payload above in the email field. Submit the form.
Then open base_url/image?filename=output.txt in the browser. The username is shown, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

Step 1: Inject the command
* It loads the feedback page and extracts the CSRF token using a regular expression.
* It sends a POST request to /feedback/submit with the token and the payload in the email field.
* The payload writes the output of whoami into output.txt.

Step 2: Read the output
* It sends a GET request to /image with filename set to output.txt.
* The params argument adds filename to the URL query string, which is where the server reads it.
* The response body is the output of whoami, which is the answer to the lab.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The server passes user input straight into a shell command. Shells support redirection, so an attacker can
write command output into any folder the web server can write to. If that folder is also publicly served,
the attacker can read the output back through the browser.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not pass user input into shell commands. Use the language's built-in functions for the task instead.
If a command must be used, pass user input as a separate, escaped argument, never as part of the command string.

Other good practices:

Validate input, allowing only the characters an email address can contain.
Keep writable folders separate from folders that are publicly served.
Run the application with the lowest permissions it needs.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Output redirection can turn a blind injection into a readable one by saving output to a file.
Any writable folder that is publicly served becomes a way to exfiltrate data.
Never trust input from a user, even when the output is hidden from them.
